.TH SEVERANCE 1 "2026-08" "severance" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
severance \- kernel-enforced work/personal boundary
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B severance
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B work
.RI [ "enter " [ profile ]]
.br
.B work run
.RI [ profile ]
.B --
.I command ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B severance
provisions and audits a kernel-enforced boundary between confidential
(zero-data-retention) work and everything else on a single machine. Each
confidential context is a
.I profile
(an enclave): a Unix group, a sealed work tree owned
.BR root:<group> " mode " 2770
with a default ACL, and a matching account. A personal process, lacking the
group, is denied entry by the filesystem, not by convention.
.PP
.B work
enters an enclave: it acquires the group for the session via a human
.BR sudo (8)
prompt and runs a shell (or one command) with the enclave's account and
environment. Nothing persists.
.PP
.B severance
answers questions about the boundary;
.B work
performs the one privileged action and answers none. "Which enclave is this
process in" is
.BR "severance current" ,
so an integrator needs to know about one command rather than two.
.SH WORK
.B work
takes a closed verb set, an optional profile in a fixed slot, and
.B --
before any command. Nothing is guessed: the verb and the profile may both be
omitted, but an unrecognised word is a usage error rather than a command.
.TP
.B work \fR[\fBenter \fR[\fIprofile\fR]]
Enter the enclave and run an interactive shell in its tree, with its group,
account and prompt. Bare
.B work
is
.BR "work enter" .
.TP
.B work run \fR[\fIprofile\fR] \fB-- \fICMD\fR ...
Run one command in the enclave and exit with its status. The
.B --
is mandatory, so no name lookup decides whether the first word is a profile.
.PP
The profile may be omitted when exactly one is provisioned or a default marker
is set. With several and no default it is REQUIRED, and
.B work
fails loud rather than guessing a group.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install \fR/\fB uninstall
Standalone only: symlink the package into
.I ~/.local
Links ONLY: it configures no other tool. A guarded no-op under a host that
already symlinks the package.
.TP
.B list
List provisioned profiles;
.B *
marks the resolved default.
.TP
.B current \fR[\fIPID\fR]
Print the profile name of the enclave the process is in, or nothing. This is a
value, not a predicate, so a caller tests the output for emptiness. Exit 0
means ANSWERED, and empty output then means "not in an enclave", which is not
an error. Exit 2 means it could NOT answer: the pid is malformed, or names no
live process. That distinction matters because a mangled pid which silently
printed nothing would report a process that IS behind the boundary as
personal, and a false negative is the one direction a ZDR wall must never fail
in. It SCANS every provisioned profile
rather than resolving one, so a process in a non-default profile's group is
still reported. Where nested enclaves leave a process holding several groups,
the innermost wins: the group
.BR "sudo -g" " made primary."
.TP
.B guard
Exit 1, with a message on stderr, when the process is inside a work tree
WITHOUT the enclave group; else exit 0. The kernel wall is the real
enforcement; this is the fail-loud reminder, called by whatever needs to be
refused before it touches work files.
.TP
.BI show " [profile]"
Print the resolved settings for a profile (or the default). A HUMAN report for
debugging a record: read it, do not parse it.
.TP
.BI use " profile" "\fR|\fB --clear"
Set (or clear) the default-profile marker.
.TP
.BI init " name " [key=val ...]
Scaffold a new profile record under
.IR ~/.config/severance/profiles/ .
.TP
.BI seal " [profile...]"
Provision the boundary (group, sealed tree + ACLs, git identity). Privileged.
.TP
.BI runner " [profile...]"
Provision the per-enclave rootless-docker runner. Privileged.
.TP
.BI forget " profile " \fR[ \fB\-\-purge \fR]
Decommission a profile: tear down its runner (reversible). With
.B \-\-purge
also remove the record and regenerate the git fragment; the work tree is left
sealed. Privileged.
.TP
.BR check " [" \-\-seal | \-\-runner ]
Audit the live boundary; non-zero on drift. Bare checks both and validates the
records.
.TP
.BI validate " [profile]"
Lint profile records (parse, valid group, work_dir a subdir) before they
provision.
.TP
.B doctor
Whole-boundary health in one report: installation, records, integrations
(our own git identity fragment), and the live wall + runner.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/severance/profiles/<name>
Profile records (key=value).
.TP
.I ~/.config/severance/default
The active-profile marker (written by
.BR "severance use" ).
.TP
.I ~/.config/git/work-context.gen
The generated git identity fragment.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WORK_PROFILE
Select a profile (overridden by an explicit argument).
.TP
.B SEVERANCE_PROFILES_DIR
Override the profiles directory (a host points this at its records).
.SH MACHINE INTERFACE
.B current
and
.B guard
are the STABLE contracts consumed by other tools: their output shape and exit
codes will not change without a major version bump. Every other command is a
human report or a mutation and is free to change its wording.
.PP
There is deliberately nothing else.
.B "show --shell"
was a third such contract, an eval-able dump of every
.BR WC_* ,
and it had no consumers: every integrator reaches severance through
.B current
and
.BR guard ,
which are one word and one exit code. It is retired, because a promise nobody
uses still pins every internal name as public API. Exported internals are not
an interface.
.PP
.B "severance context"
was the earlier spelling of this seam, in a consumer's vocabulary rather than
severance's. It is retired and now exits 1 \(em NOT the conventional 2 for a
usage error, because 2 means "warn, then proceed" in a veto contract and a
retired boundary verb must fail CLOSED \(em with a pointer:
.B "context resolve"
became
.BR current ", " "context guard" " became " guard .
Note that
.B current
prints NOTHING outside an enclave where
.B "context resolve"
printed
.IR personal .
.SH NOTES
The wall is the kernel. Permission lists and git config are not the boundary and
must not be relied on for it. The enclave group is transient: the login user is
never a permanent member. Entering the enclave is always a human sudo prompt.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudo (8),
.BR setfacl (1),
.BR git-config (1)
